    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d806-a3">

                <pb facs="0838" n="788" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg788"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 3 in No. 145.—Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Henderson to Mr.
                            Bartleman.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ciudad Bolivar</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-05-02"><hi rendition="#italic">May 2,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Your telegram of yesterday’s date received. Agent refuses to give bond and
                    steamer continues detained, for which he holds the Venezuelan sover
                    [Government?]. One of the branches of the Orinoco and opening into the Gulf of
                    Paria, is the only available route practicable for such river boats as the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bolivar</hi>, which, on account of her build, can not
                    venture on the sea. This steamer was built in Wilmington, Del., by the General
                    Steamship Company of that place, by which she is run, and is under the American
                    flag.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Henderson</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">U. S. Consular Agent.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
